2026-11-04  Roman Senn  <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>

	Icons rebuilt from the new src/svg/favicon.svg, equations typeset
	with TeX metrics from a generated Computer Modern glyph set, and an
	Advanced mode that takes the L and C of the tank as networks of
	parts (selectable in the schematic and in the equation).

	* src/network-ui.js (ShowNetworks, FormatSpan), src/app.html,
	src/stylesheets/main.css:
	L, C and f of the tank are shown as a table, one row per quantity,
	in the style of the basic mode (the black letter cell, the orange,
	green and purple name cell as wide as the widest, a boxed value on
	the same line), instead of one line of text. The rows are built
	from a list (ShowResult), so rows for the single parts can follow.
	The table is only as wide as its widest value. A span is written
	`7.3 – 89.3 pF`, with the unit once when both ends share it; the
	block is hidden while the networks are incomplete.

	* src/network-ui.js (ShowNetworks), src/app.html, test:
	L, C and f are shown for both ends of the knob when a part is a
	range (`C = 7.3pF..89.3pF   f = 30.7MHz..107.5MHz`). The example
	in the C field is now the AM oscillator section of the Mitsumi
	PVC-2LXT-L5 polyvaricon (also the CBM-443DF): 82 pF swing on a
	3.8 pF minimum, `Cosc=3.8p..85.8p`, in parallel with its trimmer
	(0 to 7 pF, set to the middle, `Ctrim=3.5p`).

	* src/equations.js (AdjustableMark, layoutPart), test:
	A part with a range is drawn as adjustable with the trimmer
	symbol: a diagonal line through the plates (or the coil), as thick
	as the leads and reaching past them, ending in a short bar across
	its upper end. A coil also gets a dotted line beside it, the
	ferrite core. The labels of a coil move over to make room.

	* src/equations.js (layoutParallel), test:
	With an odd number of parallel branches the port is placed at the
	midpoint of the outer ones, which lands a pixel or two beside the
	middle branch, so the rail got two overlapping junction dots at
	the top and at the bottom. A branch within 10 units of the port
	now takes over the port position and shares its dot.

	* src/equations.js (Parse, ComponentNode, Label):
	A part value `a..b` (`Cosc=3.8p..85.8p`) makes the part adjustable
	by a knob: ComponentNode#range holds [min, max], `value` stays
	the maximum so everything that evaluates a network keeps working.
	Parse() rejects a range that is reversed or starts at 0, toText()
	writes it back, and the schematic labels it `3.8..85.8pF`.

	* src/favicon.png, src/favicon.ico:
	Regenerated from the new src/svg/favicon.svg (16 px PNG; ICO with
	16, 32, 48, 64, 128 and 256 px layers stored as PNG, 28 KB).

	* electron/main.js (CreateWindow), electron/icon.png [new]:
	The window icon was src/favicon.png, 16x16, which the window
	manager scaled up into a blur. It is now electron/icon.png, a
	256x256 render of src/svg/favicon.svg (electron/ is part of the
	packaged files).

	* src-tauri/icons/*, resources/icons/512x512.png:
	Regenerated from src/svg/favicon.svg with `tauri icon` (the
	Android/iOS sets and 64x64.png are dropped to keep the bundle
	slim). tauri.conf.json already lists these files, so the Tauri
	app and its bundles now use the new icon; Electron builds use
	resources/icons/512x512.png.

	* tools/favicon.sh [new]:
	Rebuilds both files from src/svg/favicon.svg with rsvg-convert and
	a small python ICO writer, and also the Tauri, resources and
	Electron icons, so every icon follows the SVG.

	* src/equations.js (Part, layoutPart, Sym):
	Every part is drawn as <g data-part="name"> with a transparent
	hit rectangle, in the schematic and in the equations, so a page
	can select it in both drawings at once.

	* src/equations.js (Text, Glyph, Script, Sym, Op, Frac, Paren, Sqrt,
	ThomsonEquation#renderSVG, #toLaTeX, Network#toLaTeX):
	The equation typesetter drew <text> in whatever Times the machine
	had, with guessed glyph widths, a hand-drawn radical and
	parentheses, and a dot between L and C. It now draws Computer
	Modern outlines (src/glyphs.js) as paths with TeX metrics: axis
	and rule thickness, numerator/denominator shifts and clearance,
	delimiter sizes picked like TeX, a radical from the font, sub- and
	superscripts, operator spacing. The output no longer depends on
	installed fonts. L C is now written as a juxtaposition, as in the
	old images.
	- renderSVG(target) typesets f, L or C (renderFrequencySVG,
	  renderInductanceSVG and renderCapacitanceSVG are thin wrappers);
	  composed networks appear as factors, sums in parentheses, and as
	  L_eq / C_eq on the left side.
	- toLaTeX() gives the same equations (and Z(j omega) of a network)
	  as LaTeX source, the input of tools/equations.js.

	* src/glyphs.js [new], tools/glyphs.js [new]:
	Generated glyph atlas (digits, Latin and Greek letters, operators,
	delimiters in the five TeX sizes, radical) from MathJax's TeX font,
	91 KB. `bun run glyphs` rebuilds it.

	* src/svg/{freq,inductance,capacitance}-equation.svg,
	tools/equations.js [new]:
	The three basic-mode images are now rendered by MathJax (SVG
	output, plain paths) from ThomsonEquation#toLaTeX(), so they
	follow the model. `bun run equations` regenerates them;
	RenderLaTeX() in the same file renders any LaTeX string.

	* package.json, bun.lock:
	Dev dependency mathjax-full (build time only, not packaged);
	scripts `equations` and `glyphs`.

	* test/equations.test.js:
	Test for the glyph-based SVG output and the LaTeX source.

	* src/network-ui.js [new], src/app.html, src/app.js (SetAdvanced,
	Init), src/stylesheets/main.css:
	Advanced mode, a second view next to the Thomson calculator. An
	"Advanced" checkbox (top right) hides the basic view (class
	`basic`). A tank has one inductance and one capacitance, each
	possibly composed of several parts, but never mixed, so there are
	two fields, L and C, each a network of its own kind (`+` series,
	`|` parallel, parentheses): L `(100n | 47n) + 250n`, C
	`(27p + 470p) | 5p`. Bare values name themselves (L1, L2, ...;
	a named part like Ltrim=5u is kept), and a part of the wrong
	kind (C1 in the L field) is an error shown under the fields.
	- The tank is drawn as a schematic next to the Thomson equation
	  f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(L C)) with the two networks as factors, and
	  L, C and f are calculated when all parts have values, printed
	  like the basic mode does, without a space (281.9728nH).
	- The text of both fields and the mode are kept in the saved
	  config; the first time the fields are seeded with the values of
	  the basic mode when they are plain numbers.
	- A value may carry its unit or prefix after a space (`10 u`,
	  `4.7 nF`, `Ltrim = 5 u`); it used to be split into two tokens
	  and failed with "unexpected 'u'". The tokenizer of the fields
	  (TOKENS) joins them and Expand() hands Parse() the compact form.
	- The code is loaded with import() on first use, so basic mode
	  loads nothing extra.
	- A part can be selected, by clicking it in the schematic or in
	  the equation: both drawings mark it (hovering marks it red,
	  the selection purple) and a panel shows its name and value.
	  Enter a frequency and Solve gives the value of that part that
	  puts the tank on it (Network#solve, the other parts keep their
	  values); Use writes it into the field of the part, whether
	  that was a bare value, a named one or one without a value
	  (Scan, SetValue). The selection survives edits as long as the
	  part exists.
	- test/equations.test.js: tests for the text scanning, value
	  replacement, units after a space and solving a part.

2026-11-03  Roman Senn  <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>

	Calculator reworked and packaged. Every field takes a range, a fixed
	L or C is solved against a tuning range, the app builds for Electron
	and Tauri, and src/equations.js now models, draws and solves composed
	LC networks. Several bugs fixed, among them frequencies that came out
	halved. 26 bun tests pass (bun test test/).

	* src/app.js (Thompson.calculate):
	Remove the stray `* 2`. The formula is f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(L C));
	every frequency the calculator printed was half of that.
	Thompson.inverse, which solves L and C from a frequency, was right.

	* src/numbers.js (Unit, ParseRange, NumericValue, FormatNumber,
	RoundTo, Exponent):
	  - Unit and ParseRange share one parser. Empty or invalid input is
	    NaN or [] instead of 0, which used to count as a valid value.
	  - Ranges: `a - b`, `a..b`, `a~b` and en/em dash. `1e-6` is no
	    longer split at its minus sign. A first value without a prefix
	    takes the second one's (`2-20pF`).
	  - `100hz` is no longer hecto-hertz; Hz, H, F and a lone h are
	    base units.
	  - NumericValue and FormatNumber shift the decimal exponent
	    exactly, so 4.7e-9 * 1e9 no longer prints 4.700000000000001.
	    Exponent comes from toExponential, not Math.log10 (which
	    misjudges 1e-7).
	  - FormatNumber floors the prefix (100 nH, not 0.1 uH) and carries
	    into the next prefix when rounding reaches 1000 (999.9996 kHz
	    -> 1 MHz). A value of 0 prints as 0.
	  - RoundTo drops trailing zeros (2.000 -> 2, which it left alone
	    before).
	  - ParseRange moved here from app.js.

	* src/app.js (Init, OnInput, SetupFields, GuessField, SaveConfig,
	GetRange, ChangePrecision, CopyToClipboard):
	  - Init runs on DOMContentLoaded; the document 'load' listener
	    never fired and a 100 ms timer papered over it.
	  - Enter no longer reloads the page (the form had no submit
	    handler). Fields recalculate on input, and selecting another
	    output field recalculates.
	  - OnInput takes the event as a parameter instead of the global
	    `event`.
	  - SaveConfig stores every field; an emptied field used to keep
	    its stale saved value and come back on reload. GuessField picks
	    the single empty field, else frequency.
	  - GetRange always parses the field (an unconditional throw made
	    its cache dead code). ReadFields covers all three fields.
	  - Double-click copies once (two handlers fired), failures are
	    reported instead of unhandled. Precision is clamped to 0..9 on
	    the wheel, default 3.
	  - SeriesCaps called a ParseNumber that does not exist.

	* src/thomson.js [new] (Thompson, Cover), src/app.js (CalcFixed,
	SetResult, SetNote, FormatRange):
	With a frequency band and a tuning range on the other component
	(87.5-108 MHz with a 2-20 pF trimmer) the output used to be the
	corner combinations, i.e. an adjustable L. Cover(f, Y, tol) now gives
	the interval of one fixed X that lets Y cover the band, its geometric
	centre as the best X, whether it is feasible, and the band the best X
	really tunes. The UI shows that single value and a note below the
	fields: the valid interval, or a warning that the range is too small.
	Symmetric for L and C. With one range input the output is still the
	range it requires.

	* src/app.js (GetRange, SetResult), src/thomson.js (Cover):
	Switching the output field fed the rounded text of the previous
	result back in as input, so a band that fitted exactly could be
	reported as too small (0.0005% short). Computed fields now remember
	the unrounded numbers behind their text and use them until the text
	is edited. Cover also takes a relative tolerance and reports `exact`;
	the UI passes 10^-precision (at most 1%) and says "just covers the
	band, within rounding" instead of warning. Genuine misses still warn.

	* src/app.html, src/stylesheets/main.css:
	Add the #note line under the precision slider. Its text is
	selectable; the rest of the page keeps user-select: none.

	* src/equations.js:
	Rewritten around one idea: a network is a two-terminal
	series/parallel tree of inductors and capacitors, with no adjustable
	parts in the model. ComponentNode, SeriesGroup, ParallelGroup and
	ThomsonEquation keep working.
	  - Parse('(L1=27n | C1=470p) + C2=3p') builds a network,
	    toString() round-trips it. `+` is series, `|` parallel.
	  - resonances(): series and parallel resonances, found by scanning
	    the reactance for sign changes and bisecting. A rise through
	    zero is a series, a fall a parallel resonance. Cells that fall
	    without a sign change are refined, so close zero/pole pairs are
	    found.
	  - solve(name, f, values, {type, index}): bisect a component value
	    so a resonance lands on f. band(name, [lo, hi]): the band a
	    resonance covers while that component is swept.
	  - schematic(): small vertical schematics (plates, coils, junction
	    dots, ports or ground, name over value). equationSVG(): the
	    impedance Z(jw) typeset with fractions.
	    ThomsonEquation.renderFrequencySVG() uses the same typesetter
	    and brackets sums correctly.
	  - evaluate(), toJS() keep their meaning for networks of one kind
	    of part; mixed networks throw. drawCircuit() and toSVG()
	    changed output.

	* src/favicon.ico, src/svg/:
	favicon.ico 361 KB -> 23 KB: the same six sizes as PNG layers.
	src/svg was a symlink to ../app/svg, which a static host would not
	serve; it is a real directory now.

	* electron/main.js [new; was src/main.js]:
	Electron 45.2.1 shell that loads src/app.html as it is hosted.
	Sandboxed, no node integration, external links open in the browser.
	`electron .` uses its own "(dev)" profile directory, so development
	runs no longer share saved fields (localStorage) with an installed
	build.

	* package.json, bun.lock:
	Bun scripts: start, web, test, electron:pack, electron:dist,
	tauri:dev, tauri:build, build. electron-builder config: the deb
	installs to /opt/thomson-calculator (productName thomson-calculator),
	icon and menu entry named thomson-calculator, output in release/. The
	Tauri JS packages are dev dependencies, so the Electron bundle no
	longer contains them.

	* src-tauri/ (Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock [new], tauri.conf.json,
	src/lib.rs):
	  - Package renamed from `app`: /usr/bin/thomson-calculator and
	    thomson-calculator.png icons; real maintainer, description,
	    licence and homepage instead of the template's.
	  - The window loads app.html; Tauri's default index.html does not
	    exist, so every earlier bundle showed "asset not found:
	    index.html".
	  - Release profile: opt-level s, LTO, one codegen unit, panic
	    abort, strip. tauri-plugin-log, log, serde and serde_json
	    removed; Tauri without its default features except wry, x11 and
	    common-controls-v6. Binary 11.1 MB -> 3.2 MB, deb and rpm 2.85
	    MiB -> 1.31 MiB.
	  - AppImage (81 MiB, it bundles WebKitGTK) is not in the default
	    targets: bun run tauri:build -- --bundles appimage.
	  - beforeBuildCommand (bun build) removed, beforeDevCommand is bun
	    run web.

	* app/, build/, e2e/, config/, src/hello_world/, src/helpers/,
	src/menu/ [removed]:
	The unused webpack, electron-forge and spectron boilerplate,
	including a stale built copy of app.js. Nothing referenced it except
	tauri.conf.json, which now points at ../src.

	* test/numbers.test.js, test/thomson.test.js, test/equations.test.js
	[new]:
	bun tests: parsing and formatting, the fixed-part solver (rounded
	inputs, exact fits, genuine misses), and the network model against
	closed forms, including a random-network check that the automatic
	frequency range loses nothing but zero/pole pairs closer than one
	scan cell.

	* README.md, CLAUDE.md, ChangeLog [new]:
	README.md rewritten (usage, ranges, running, building, equations.js).
	CLAUDE.md now says to keep this ChangeLog. ChangeLog [new] is this
	file.

2026-11-02  Roman Senn  <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>

	* src/app.js, src/numbers.js, src/equations.js, src/formula.js:
	Split the single-file app into modules: numbers.js (parsing and
	formatting of values), equations.js (components, series and parallel
	groups, the Thomson equation) and app.js (the page). formula.js was
	added and merged back the same night. Further refactoring of
	numbers.js and equations.js followed on 2026-11-03.
